10-605 Mini-project: one model from four experts

Work in groups of 2 or 3. Deadlines, grade weights, the late policy and the competition bonus are
announced on the course site.


THE TASK

We trained a GPT-2 small model (124M parameters) partway on a general web mix: the seed. Continuing the
seed on one domain gave four experts: math (ClimbMix cluster 01), code (11), medicine (17), and history
and travel (03). Each is strong on its own domain and weak on the others. Continuing the seed the same
amount on the general mix gave the generalist.

Build one model with the lowest average loss over the four domains, within the rules below. The best
single expert averages 3.33 on the dev set, while scoring each domain with its own expert would give
2.78. How much of that gap one model can recover, and how, is the project.


DELIVERABLES

1. Proposal (10%): your group, at least 3 distinct methods, your hypotheses, and an experiment plan
   with the A10G-hours each experiment should cost. One submission per group.
2. Final (90%): a leaderboard submission, your code, and a report of at most 2 pages (PDF):
   - methodology (20 pts): Which 3+ methods did you implement? Describe each briefly, including its
     objective, constraints, and how it uses the experts and the pool.
   - comparison (60 pts): Compare every method against the baselines. Which gave the best dev loss?
     Which was easiest to implement? Which offered the best quality-cost tradeoff? Include a table of
     cross entropy, perplexity, loss difference, parameters, active parameters and training
     A10G-hours. Plot dev loss against training compute, and give paired bootstrap intervals (over dev
     windows) for loss differences. Include ablations and unsuccessful results.
   - reflection (10 pts): Did the results match what you expected? What did you learn about combining
     experts? Discuss limitations, possible overfitting, and one follow-up experiment.
Changing only a random seed, a learning rate or the training length does not make a new method.


SETUP (AWS g5.xlarge, one A10G GPU)

    pip install --no-cache-dir torch==2.10.0 numpy huggingface_hub
    python3 -c "import torch; print(torch.cuda.is_available())"   # must print True
    git clone https://github.com/aditya-gupta-04/10605-f26-btm-miniproj.git
    cd 10605-f26-btm-miniproj
    python3 code/download_data.py

Keep torch at 2.10.0: newer builds need a newer NVIDIA driver than the g5 images have, and PyTorch then
cannot see the GPU. download_data.py fetches models/ and pool/ from Hugging Face (about 2 GB) and
checks every file against MANIFEST.sha256. Run long jobs inside tmux so they survive an SSH disconnect.

    models/seed.bin, models/generalist.bin, models/expert_<domain>.bin   the six provided models
    pool/<domain>.bin            training data: 60M tokens per domain, never seen by the experts
    dev/<domain>.bin             dev set: about 1M tokens per domain
    code/gpt.py                  the models' architecture: load, run and save the provided checkpoints
    code/data.py                 read the token files (GPT-2 token ids)
    code/create_submission.py    check a solution, score it on the dev set, write submission.log
    solutions/                   two placeholder solutions to copy and change


STARTER CODE

    python3 solutions/average_experts.py --out runs/average   # no training: the experts averaged
    python3 solutions/short_training.py --out runs/short      # the generalist plus one extra layer,
                                                              #   200 steps on an equal mix of the pools
    python3 code/create_submission.py runs/short              # check, score on dev, write submission.log

short_training.py is the place to start your own training script. It is split into labeled sections
(settings, model and weight loading, data, optimizer, train loop, saving), and --steps, --batch-size,
--micro-batch, --lr and --warmup are plain arguments.

A solution writes an output directory containing:

    model_def.py   defines load_model(directory, device), returning a torch.nn.Module that maps token
                   ids [batch, length] (int64) to logits [batch, length, 50257]
    your weights   in any format
    train.log      your training log, in any format

Any architecture is allowed: put its class in model_def.py (or in files next to it) so load_model can
rebuild it. solutions/gpt_model_def.py loads the provided architecture, and
solutions/extra_layer_model_def.py shows a changed one. When saving in the provided format, use gpt.save
or gpt.write_checkpoint (they keep the models' muP settings). Train with gpt.optimizer_groups (the
per-group learning rates).


RULES

1. Data: train only on pool/, in any mix or order, plus text the provided models generate inside your
   final recipe. No other data: no other ClimbMix data, no dev tokens, no outside text.
2. Models: only the provided ones, used in any way. No other pretrained models or model APIs.
3. Your model: any architecture, at most 300M total parameters and at most 150M active parameters per
   token, causal (each prediction depends only on earlier tokens). create_submission.py checks the
   total and causality, and reports an estimate of active parameters (about 124M for the provided
   models); staff check the 150M limit for the top groups.
4. Compute: your final recipe is one script from the provided files to your output directory. It must
   finish in 2 hours on a fresh g5.xlarge, everything included. Earlier experiments may enter it only
   as settings written into it (at most 1 MB), not as weights, cached model outputs or generated data.
5. Dev set: use it to compare and choose, never to train.
6. Code: Python and PyTorch; torch.compile and custom kernels are fine.


EVALUATION AND SUBMITTING

Score: the mean next-token cross-entropy (nats, bf16) over non-overlapping 2,048-token windows of each
domain, averaged over the four domains. Lower is better.

    python3 code/create_submission.py <output directory>

Upload <output directory>/submission.log, your train.log and your code (final recipe and model_def.py)
to Gradescope. The leaderboard shows the dev score from submission.log.

Final ranking: after the deadline, staff re-run the final recipes of the top 3 groups on a fresh
g5.xlarge with only the provided files and no network, and score the results on a hidden test set of
never-released ClimbMix text (about 1M tokens per domain). A group drops out if its recipe takes longer
than 2 hours, breaks a rule (including the 150M active-parameter limit), or gives a dev score more than
0.005 away from the reported one, and the next group is re-run in its place. Run-to-run noise is about
0.001, so fix your random seeds.

Baselines (dev score):

    best single expert (math)                                   3.331
    average of the four experts (average_experts.py)            3.516
    generalist                                                  3.156
